<article class="help-article p-4">
    <header class="help-article-header mb-4">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined help-article-icon">waving_hand</span>
        <h1 class="help-article-title">Welcome to Singularity</h1>
        <p class="help-article-lead">
            Singularity is the authoring environment for the Sabatier stack &mdash; an IDE for designing
            data models and generating the PHP web service project that backs them.
        </p>
    </header>

    <p>
        With Singularity you design your application's data model visually &mdash; its entities, their
        attributes, and the relationships between them &mdash; and Singularity generates a Sabatier Service
        backend from that model: the project structure and the managed-object classes, with none of that
        scaffolding written by hand. From the moment you generate, the service already answers requests for
        your entities. Business logic and a frontend are what you add on top.
    </p>

    <h2 class="help-heading">What you can do</h2>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li>Create and open projects, each backed by a folder on disk.</li>
        <li>Design a model made of <strong>entities</strong>, <strong>attributes</strong>, <strong>relationships</strong>, fetch requests, composite types, and configurations.</li>
        <li>Visualize your model as an interactive entity-relationship graph.</li>
        <li>Save model files, generate managed-object subclasses, inspect SQL output, and import existing models.</li>
        <li>Use the AI Copilot to ask questions or make model changes in the selected context.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="help-heading">From model to running service</h2>
    <p>
        What you generate is a <strong>Sabatier Service</strong> backend, and how far you take it is up to you.
        The same design tool spans the whole range:
    </p>
    <ul class="help-list">
        <li><strong>A working API with no code.</strong> Once you model your entities and generate the project, Sabatier Service already answers HTTP requests for them &mdash; listing, filtering, creating, updating, and deleting records &mdash; with the access control you defined in the model applied automatically. You write no endpoints to get a usable API from your design.</li>
        <li><strong>A backend with your own logic.</strong> When you need more, add responders and domain code alongside the generated model and subclasses.</li>
        <li><strong>A full-stack app with any frontend.</strong> The backend exposes a clear HTTP contract, so the frontend is free &mdash; Singularity's own approach, or a framework such as Vue or React.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="help-callout">
        <span class="material-symbols-outlined">lightbulb</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Self-referential by design.</strong>
            Singularity was modeled in its own editor: the app you are using was designed as a Sabatier
            model, saved, and generated the same way any project you create here is. It is a working
            proof of what you can build with the projects it generates &mdash; not an example, the real thing.
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="help-heading">Where to next</h2>
    <p>
        If this is your first time here, start by
        <a class="help-link" href="#create-project" data-help-anchor="create-project">creating your first project</a>.
        Once you have a project open, learn how to
        <a class="help-link" href="#editor" data-help-anchor="editor">navigate the Editor</a>.
    </p>
</article>
